editorialMd:
Build the result list

Each point in this graph is a task. A path from task a to task b means a must happen before b. Put all tasks with no paths coming into them in a queue, in number order. A queue serves the task at its front first. Remove that task, record its number, and remove its outgoing paths. Check its next tasks in number order. Add a task to the back of the queue as soon as it has no paths left coming in. If you cannot record every task, the rules contain a loop: use an empty result list instead.

Return the answer

Count the numbers in the result list that are at least k (greater than or equal to k). Return that count. An empty list gives 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(V^2); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length; V is the number of graph points. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function dispatchOrderThresholdFile(links, k) {
  const a=links; let out=[];
  const n=a.length,degree=Array(n).fill(0),queue=[];for(const row of a)for(let v=0;v<n;v++)degree[v]+=row[v];for(let v=0;v<n;v++)if(degree[v]===0)queue.push(v);for(let h=0;h<queue.length;h++){const u=queue[h];out.push(u);for(let v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]&&--degree[v]===0)queue.push(v);}if(out.length!==n)out=[];
  return out.filter(x=>x>=k).length;
}


